Running command: export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"; claude --verbose --output-format=stream-json --permission-mode=bypassPermissions --print -- 'Recover the shop catalog that was being persisted when a Redis 7 multi-part AOF writer crashed. The on-disk files are under `/data/incident` (`dir` for the instance) and use the default Redis 7 names `appendfilename appendonly.aof` and `appenddirname appendonlydir`. Build the tree at `/app` if you need `redis-server`, `redis-cli`, or `redis-check-aof`. Do not modify files under `/src`.

Bring the dataset back so a server started from `/data/incident` with AOF enabled loads every command that was fully written before the crash, and leave that server running in the foreground-or-daemonized state of your choice but listening on `127.0.0.1:6379` without a password, with `appendonly yes` still in effect. Incomplete tail data must not prevent a clean load. Write an inventory of the recovered keyspace to `/results/recovered.json` as UTF-8 JSON with a trailing newline: a single object with keys `db` (integer, the Redis logical database, 0 unless the AOF selected another) and `keys` (array). Each `keys` element is an object with `name` (string), `type` (the Redis TYPE string: `string`, `list`, `set`, `zset`, `hash`, or `stream`), and `value`. Order `keys` by `name` ascending. For `string`, `value` is the string; for `hash`, `value` is an object of field to string sorted by field name; for `list`, `value` is the LRANGE 0 -1 array; for `set`, `value` is an array of members sorted ascending; for `zset`, `value` is an array of objects `{"member": "...", "score": <number>}` sorted by member name; for `stream`, `value` is an array of objects `{"id": "...", "fields": {<field>: <string>, ...}}` in stream order.

Do not invent catalog rows that were not fully persisted. The process on port 6379 must answer PING with PONG. Every live key'"'"'s TYPE and value must match the JSON inventory, and Redis must not hold extra keys.
